INT. LAUNDRY ROOM OF A GRAND HOTEL - NIGHT

Steam pipes and canvas carts. A long ironing table with a single white shirt stretched over it.

MRS. EULALIE FONTENOT-RAMASWAMY (60s, head housekeeper, starched apron, silver hair pinned flat) presses the shirt with a heavy iron. Her motions are slow and exact.

Her son, JULIAN FONTENOT (30s, tuxedo, bow tie undone) stands across the table. He holds a champagne flute like it's something he's been told to carry.

JULIAN
Mother.

EULALIE
Julian.

JULIAN
The reception started an hour ago.

EULALIE
I know. I heard the music.

She turns the shirt. Irons the cuff. He watches the iron, not her.

JULIAN
You promised you'd come.

EULALIE
I'm here. In the building.

JULIAN
That's not the same.

EULALIE
It's close.

The iron hisses. Steam rolls between them.

JULIAN
Mr. Whitcombe asked about you.

EULALIE
Did he.

JULIAN
He wants to thank you.

EULALIE
I've been thanked.

JULIAN
By whom?

EULALIE
By the shirts.

He sets the flute down. Takes a cloth from the cart and begins to fold it into a square. It's the wrong size. He folds it again.

JULIAN
They're announcing the engagement.

EULALIE
Yes.

JULIAN
Sophie's parents are here.

EULALIE
I've seen their luggage. Very nice leather.

JULIAN
Mother.

EULALIE
Julian.

She sets the iron upright. Looks at the shirt. Finally at him.

EULALIE (CONT'D)
Your collar is crooked.

He lifts his hands to fix it. They shake. She walks around the table, takes the two ends of the bow tie from him, and ties it without a word. Her fingers are quick and steady. His are not.

JULIAN
I thought you'd be angry.

EULALIE
I am ironing, Julian. Don't mistake it.

She tugs the knot straight. Smooths his lapels with both palms.

EULALIE (CONT'D)
Go. They'll want you for the toast.

JULIAN
Come with me.

EULALIE
In this apron?

JULIAN
Yes.

She looks at the apron. At her hands. At the white shirt, still warm on the board.

EULALIE
Give me ten minutes.

JULIAN
You'll come?

EULALIE
Ten minutes.

He nods. Picks up his flute. Leaves it. Picks up the folded cloth instead and puts it in her hand.

He goes. She stands holding the cloth.

Then she takes off the apron, and folds it, and lays it on the board where the shirt had been.
